NOT a backport — this fix does not exist upstream. Blocks using `lua@5.3a` as a device
on the `on/request` / `on/response` hooks, which is what the write-gate design needs.

`dev_lua:do_encode/2` passes any term it does not recognise straight through to
`luerl:encode/2`, whose final clause is `error({badarg, Term})`. An `{as, Device, Msg}`
subresolve directive is one such term, and it appears in the `body' of every hook message
whose request was path-composed — `POST /<id>~process@1.0/push`, i.e. every write to a
process. So a Lua device on a hook works for simple paths and crashes for exactly the
requests a gate exists to inspect.

The failure is near-invisible: the badarg is raised inside the VM call, and `dev_p4`
reports only "Could not estimate price of request." (HTTP 400) with no trace. It reads
like a bug in the Lua script. `HB_PRINT=lua_error,lua` is the only way to see the cause.

Encoded exactly as `hb_ao:subresolve/4` interprets the directive — the message with
`device` set to `DevID`. That is what the term means, and what the `<id>~process@1.0` path
syntax expands to (`hb_singleton`: `Part~Device => {as, Device, #{path => Part}}`), so a
script sees an ordinary message carrying a device. Overwriting an existing `device` is the
semantics rather than a collision, and `undefined` means "leave it alone", mirroring the
matching clause in `subresolve/4`. Guarded on `is_map` — the only shape `hb_singleton`
produces — so anything else falls through and behaves as it does today.

DELIBERATELY NARROW — do not generalise this to "render any unencodable tuple as a
string". That variant was built and tested first, and it breaks the device sandbox: the
`{error, {device_not_loadable, _}}` that a `device-sandbox` denial produces stops being a
hard failure and becomes an ordinary string the script reads and returns successfully.
`dev_lua:ao_core_sandbox_test` catches it, and softening a sandbox denial is not something
this fix needs to do.

Validated on this image at v0.9-FINAL: upstream `dev_lua` + `dev_p4` eunit 20/20 (same as
stock), D10 E2E 21/21, and an 11/11 spike proving a Lua pricing device admits an
allow-listed signer's write, refuses a non-allow-listed one at the request hook, and that
the refused write consumes no scheduler slot. Reproduce with
anyone-protocol `smart-contracts/ao/scripts/probe/p4-lua-pricing.ts`.

REBASED onto edge @ 14e9f68a (2026-08-26). The tree moved `src/dev_lua.erl` ->
`src/preloaded/vm/dev_lua.erl`; the `do_encode/2` clause list this extends is otherwise
unchanged, so the added clauses are identical. Still NOT upstream: edge has no
`do_encode({as, ...})` clause.


